Displaced Avenger wrote:
> Currently, when you press Z, you get a list of spells to cast, in the
> order that you first learned the spells (ignoring mind-flayer induced
> forgetfulness).  It'd be nice to have other options for this; I can
> imagine several:
>
> 1) Turn learned (current default).

It gives the player some feeling about when the spells will time
out.

> 2) By class and level.  So you get all attack spells in a group, all
> healing spells in a group, etc.

That's what I find to be very useful. Though, the advantage of #1
would get lost. To avoid that, maybe some toggling between the
two display modes would be appropriate, or provide the number
of turns when the spell was learned instead to avoid the overhead
of the two display modes.

> 3) Ascending fail percentage. Lists spells you're more likely to be
> able to cast first.

Maybe the best variant if coupled with #2, IMO; first by class then
by percentage.

> 4) Descending "freshness", aka most recently [re?]read first.

> 5) User-controlled, perhaps through some sort of #spelladjust command.

If you currently type a letter of a spell while inspecting the spell
list you can adjust the spells manually; that's already implemented.
Though it's a very clumsy way to reorganize spells, so I think - I
mean, I hope! - the next version will provide some improvement. My
favourite will be; first order class, second order fail percentage.

> I would prefer option #2, myself, and preferably with a constant
> lettering system (thus, force bolt would always be 'a', drain life 'b',
> etc.), which would make it easier to cast spells quickly--thus, Zf
> would always cast finger of death (assuming English alphabet and that
> the character has learned the spell).
>
> Thoughts?

I don't like the fixed letters idea from a program evolution point of
view; any future additions might require cludges or re-definition of
the letters. Then I mostly use just a couple of all learned spells
which I can easily memorize anyway.

